# Elimination of indeterminacy: Conrad's Theorem 2.4, Cases 1 and 2

This module only re-exports its parts, which live in
`WeilConjectures.Blowup.EliminationOfIndeterminacy`; it was split to keep the compilation of
each part cheap.

This module proves Cases 1 and 2 of Conrad's Theorem 2.4 (Conrad, *Deligne's notes on
Nagata compactifications*, proof of Theorem 2.4, pp. 13–16): a partial map from a
quasi-compact open to a quasi-affine target locally of finite type extends, after an
admissible blow-up, to an open neighbourhood of the preserved open with closed graph.

Over an affine base (Case 1) the core is the sub-case of a relative affine-space target,
from which the general quasi-affine target is obtained by embedding it into a relative
affine space. Case 2 globalises Case 1 to a quasi-compact quasi-separated base by
applying it over each member of a finite affine open cover, extending the local centres
to the base, blowing up the product of the extensions and gluing.

## Main results

- `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_affineSpace`: for `X` affine, `U` a
  quasi-compact open and `f : U ⟶ 𝔸(σ; X)` an `X`-morphism, there are a `U`-admissible
  blow-up `B`, an open `W` of the blow-up containing the preimage of `U`, and an
  `X`-morphism `g : W ⟶ 𝔸(σ; X)` agreeing with `f` over `U` whose graph
  `W ⟶ Bl ×_X 𝔸(σ; X)` has closed range.
  (Blueprint: `lemma:nagata-conrad-affine-space-extension`.)
- `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isQuasiAffine`: the same statement for
  a quasi-affine target `Y` of finite type over the affine base `X`, with the additional
  conclusion that the preimage of `U` is scheme-theoretically dense in the blow-up.
  (Blueprint: `lemma:nagata-conrad-quasi-affine-affine-base`.)
- `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isQuasiAffineHom`: Conrad's Theorem 2.4,
  Case 2 — the same statement for a qcqs base `X` and a separated, quasi-affine morphism
  `p : Y ⟶ X` locally of finite type.
  (Blueprint: `lemma:nagata-conrad-quasi-affine-equal-opens`.)

Two auxiliary statements feed Case 2:
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isAffineOpen` runs Case 1 over an affine
open of the base and extends the centre found there to the whole base, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_extend_of_lift` transports such a local extension, closed graph
included, along a lift of the global blow-up to the local one.

## Proof sketch

Cover `U` by finitely many basic opens `D(u)`; on `D(u)` the coordinates of `f` are
fractions `g u j / u ^ N` for a single large `N`, and after one further enlargement of
`N` the identities `g u j * u' ^ N = g u' j * u ^ N` hold *exactly* in
`A = Γ(X, ⊤)`. The centre is the ideal `I₀` generated by all `u ^ N` and all `g u j`;
it restricts to the unit ideal on `U`, so its ideal sheaf is a `U`-admissible centre.

On the blow-up chart at `u ^ N` the coordinate `j` extends as the degree-one Rees
fraction `(g u j · X) / (u ^ N · X)` — the *slope* `AffineBlowup.awaySlope` below — and
the displayed exact identities make the chart transitions hold on the nose, so the
extensions glue over the union `W` of the `u ^ N`-charts, which contains the preimage
of `U`.

Closedness of the graph is checked on the chart at each generator `a` of the centre:
there the graph is the vanishing locus of the sections `v̂ᵢ · tⱼ − ŵᵢⱼ` built from the
slopes `vᵢ = (uᵢ^N X)/(aX)` and `wᵢⱼ = (g uᵢ j · X)/(aX)`, because the slopes of the
generators of the centre generate the unit ideal on the chart
(`AffineBlowup.one_mem_span_awaySlope`).

For Case 2, the global centre is the product of the extended local centres times a
finite-type ideal cutting out the boundary `Uᶜ`
(`Scheme.exists_finiteType_ideal_coe_support_eq_compl`, Stacks Tag 01PH). Its support is
then exactly `Uᶜ`, so the inverse image of `U` in the blow-up is scheme-theoretically dense
and, in particular, topologically dense. That density is what makes the local extension
loci pairwise compatible — `Wᵢ ⊓ π ⁻¹ᵁ Vⱼ ≤ Wⱼ` — which is in turn what makes the graph of
the glued extension meet the part of `Y ×_X X'` over `Vⱼ` in the closed local graph there.

## Implementation notes

The affine-space coordinate type is any `σ : Type u` with `[Finite σ]` rather than
`Fin n`: finiteness is what the common-denominator argument needs, and this matches the
index types produced by `exists_immersion_affineSpace_of_quasiAffine'` (Stacks 04II)
with which this theorem is composed on the route to Stacks Tag 081S.

